📋 Copy an Array
Definition

Copying an array means creating another array that contains the same elements as the original array.

Example
Original: [10, 20, 30, 40]
Copy:     [10, 20, 30, 40]

The copied array should be a separate array, so changes to one should not unexpectedly affect the other.

Method 1: Using copy()
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]

copied_arr = arr.copy()

print(copied_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40]
Method 2: Using Slicing
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]

copied_arr = arr[:]

print(copied_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40]
Method 3: Using a Loop

This method is useful for understanding the copying process.

arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]

copied_arr = []

for item in arr:
    copied_arr.append(item)

print(copied_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30, 40]
Important Difference: Assignment vs Copy
Assignment
arr = [10, 20, 30]

new_arr = arr

new_arr[0] = 100

print(arr)
print(new_arr)
Output
[100, 20, 30]
[100, 20, 30]

Here, both variables refer to the same list.

Actual Copy
arr = [10, 20, 30]

new_arr = arr.copy()

new_arr[0] = 100

print(arr)
print(new_arr)
Output
[10, 20, 30]
[100, 20, 30]

Here, arr and new_arr are separate lists.

Time and Space Complexity

For copying an array with n elements:

Operation	Time Complexity	Space Complexity
Copy Array	O(n)	O(n)

Every element needs to be copied into the new array.

Key Takeaways
Copying creates a new array with the same elements.
new_arr = arr does not create an independent copy.
copy() and slicing [:] create a separate list.
Copying an array takes O(n) time and O(n) extra space.
